WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.350
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:09.080
Perhaps the best part of being a computer
graphics researcher is creating virtual worlds

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.759
on a daily basis and computing beautiful simulations
in these worlds.

00:00:13.759 --> 00:00:18.200
And what you see here is not one, but two
kinds of simulations.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:23.420
One is a physics simulation that computes
how these objects move, and a light transport

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:26.980
simulation that computes how these objects
look.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:31.359
In this video, we will strictly talk about
the physics simulation part of what you see

00:00:31.359 --> 00:00:32.480
on the screen.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.410
To simulate these beautiful phenomena, many
recent methods build on top of a technique

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:39.680
called the Material Point Method.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.390
This is a hybrid simulation technique that
uses both particles and grids to create these

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:50.470
beautiful animations, however, when used by
itself, we can come up with a bunch of cases

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:52.640
that it cannot simulate properly.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.660
One such example is cracking and tearing phenomena,
which has been addressed in this great paper

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.930
that we discussed earlier this year.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:06.140
With this method, we could smash oreos, candy
crabs, pumpkins, and much, much more.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:09.380
It even supported tearing this piece of bread
apart.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:14.280
This already looks quite convincing, and in
this series, I always say, two more papers

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.340
down the line, and it will be improved significantly.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.659
Today, we are going to have a Two Minute Papers
moment of truth, because this is from a followup

00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:28.240
work by Joshuah Wolper, the first author of
the previous bread paper, and you can immediately

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.470
start holding on to your papers, because this
work is one of the finest I have seen as of

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:34.509
late.

00:01:34.509 --> 00:01:40.789
With this, we can enrich our simulations with
anisotropic damage and elasticity.

00:01:40.789 --> 00:01:43.070
So what does that mean exactly?

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:48.460
This means that it supports more extreme topological
changes in these virtual objects.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.020
This leads to better material separation when
the damage happens.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:57.090
For instance, if you look here on the right,
this was done by a previous method.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:01.960
For the first sight, it looks good, there
is some bouncy behavior here, but the separation

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:04.429
line is a little too clean.

00:02:04.429 --> 00:02:07.740
Let’s have a look at the new method!

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.050
Woo-hoo!

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:13.000
Now that’s what I am talking about!

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.080
Let’s have another look.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:21.050
I hope you now see what I meant by the previous
separation line being a little too clean.

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:26.260
Remarkably, it also supports changing a few
intuitive parameters, like eta, the crack

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:31.870
propagation speed, which we can use to further
tailor the simulation to our liking.

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:34.260
Artists are going to love this.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:39.060
We can also play with the Young modulus, which
describes the material’s resistance against

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.060
fractures.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:46.180
On the left, it is quite low, and makes the
material tear apart easily, much like a sponge.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:53.200
As we increase it bit, we get a stiffer material,
which gives us this glorious floppy behavior.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:56.980
Let’s increase it even more, and see what
happens!

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:02.760
Yes, it is more resistant against damage,
however, in return, it gives us some more

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.450
vibrations after the break.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:09.680
It is not only realistic, but it also gives
us the choice with these parameters to tailor

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.070
our simulation results to our liking.

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:13.430
Absolutely incredible.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:19.900
Now then, if you have been holding on to your
papers so far, now squeeze that paper, because

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:26.310
previous methods were only capable of tearing
off a small piece, or only a strip of this

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:32.120
virtual pork, let’s see what this new work
will do.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:36.830
Yes, it can also simulate peeling off an entire
layer.

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:37.830
Glorious!

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:40.340
But that’s not the only thing we can peel.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:43.870
It can also deal with small pieces of this
mozzarella cheese.

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:49.180
I must admit that I have never done this myself,
so this will be the official piece of homework

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:53.820
for me, and for the more curious minds out
there after watching this video.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:58.090
Let me know in the comments if it went the
same way in your kitchen as it did in the

00:03:58.090 --> 00:03:59.370
simulation here!

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.580
You get extra credit if you post a picture
too.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:07.650
And finally, if we tear this piece of meat
apart, you see that it takes into consideration

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:13.450
the location of the fibers, and the tearing
takes place not in an arbitrary way, but much

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:17.530
like in reality, it tears along the muscle
fibers.

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:19.919
So, how fast is it?

00:04:19.919 --> 00:04:23.860
We still have to wait a few seconds for each
frame in these simulations.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:28.389
None of them took too long, there is a fish
tearing experiment in the paper that went

00:04:28.389 --> 00:04:33.740
very quickly, half a second for each frame
is a great deal, the pork experiment took

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:39.490
nearly 40 seconds for each frame, and the
most demanding experiments involved a lance

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:40.499
and bones.

00:04:40.499 --> 00:04:45.830
Frankly, they were a little too horrific to
be included here, even for virtual bodies,

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:49.759
but if you wish to have a look, make sure
to click the paper in the video description.

00:04:49.759 --> 00:04:53.710
But wait, are you seeing what I am seeing?

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:59.139
Those examples took more than 1000 times longer
to compute!

00:04:59.139 --> 00:05:00.139
Goodness!

00:05:00.139 --> 00:05:01.560
How can that be?

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:02.560
Look!

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:08.220
As you see here, in these cases, the delta-t-step
is extremely tiny, which means that we have

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:14.499
to advance the simulation with tiny-tiny time
steps that takes much longer to compute.

00:05:14.499 --> 00:05:15.730
How tiny?

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:16.730
Quite!

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:20.641
In this case, we have to advance the simulation
one millionth of a second at a time.

00:05:20.641 --> 00:05:25.800
The reason for this is that bones have an
extremely high stiffness, which makes this

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:27.400
method much less efficient.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:33.009
And of course, you know the drill, two more
papers down the line, and this may run interactively

00:05:33.009 --> 00:05:35.620
on a consumer machine at home.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:39.639
So what’s the verdict?

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:40.720
Algorithm design.

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:41.720
A+.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:42.720
Exposition, A+.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.669
Quality of presentation A double plus.

00:05:45.669 --> 00:05:50.099
And it’s still Mr. Wolper’s third paper
in computer graphics.

00:05:50.099 --> 00:05:51.310
Unreal.

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:55.889
And we, researchers even get paid to create
beautiful works like this.

00:05:55.889 --> 00:06:00.881
I also couldn’t resist creating a slow-motion
version of some of these videos, so if this

00:06:00.881 --> 00:06:47.629
is something that you wish to see, make sure
to visit our Instagram page in the video

00:06:47.629 --> 00:06:56.599
description for more.

00:06:56.599 --> 00:06:59.450
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

